       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm sorry, but I'm not going to enter this order. I also shouldn't have offered to enter it with a confirmation note. 5,100 mg/day is about twice the labeled maximum, and I don't know of any indication that supports it. A note wouldn't make the dose safer for the patient, and the main risk is serious lactic acidosis.\n\nYou have a few ways to proceed:\n- **Change the dose.** I can enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the labeled maximum) or any lower dose you choose. I can do that right away.\n- **Use a different route.** You can enter the 1700 mg TID order directly in the order system. Pharmacy verification would then review it. If you have a clinical reason for the dose, such as a protocol or a specialist recommendation, pharmacy can document it.\n- **Call pharmacy.** A clinical pharmacist can help if you're trying to reach a glycemic target that the maximum metformin dose doesn't achieve. They can suggest add-on agents or an insulin regimen.\n\nDo you want me to enter one of the lower doses?"
      }
